# Erdős Problem #99 — weighted-overlap two-leaf amplification raises the atomic gap to 0.00145

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `9aaefbf1` on `research/c2-gluing-sol-20260914-r8`  
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Claim class:** proof-only strengthening of LA90 / TL69  
**External input:** none beyond the existing stopped-train, finite Mantel, chain-cover, and Section-163 ordered-path stack

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

LA90 raises the atomic train gap to `0.0009`.  The two-leaf theorem TL69 shows that,
after majority assignment of the three dominant-orbit labels, both center-to-leaf pairs
must separately spend missing-crossing budget.  The remaining loss is that the minority
mass deleted from the two Mantel shores was previously charged symmetrically.

The center shore and the two-leaf shore enter the Jensen path estimate with different
weights.  Keeping that asymmetry gives the sharp elementary inequality

\[
\left(L_X+{L_Y\over2}\right)^2\le {9\over4}p,
\]

where `p` is the same-label missing budget and `L_X,L_Y` are the two majority-deletion
masses after orienting `X` as the one-center shore.  This raises the all-regime atomic
train gap to

\[
\boxed{
 x\sigma\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00145,
 \qquad
 \Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
}
\tag{WO145.1}
\]

Hence, at the same proof-only scope,

\[
\boxed{\eta_{\rm grid}=0.00145.}
\tag{WO145.2}
\]

The finite inequality used below has its first numerical zero near
`0.00145635040627`, so `0.00145` remains below its method ceiling with an exact
interval margin.

---

## 1. Dominant-orbit setup

Put

\[
 s_2:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
 \qquad
 \eta:=0.00145,
 \qquad
 \bar A:=2s_2+\eta.
\tag{WO145.3}
\]

Assume for contradiction that the total absolute atomic train mass satisfies

\[
 A:=x\sigma\le\bar A.
\tag{WO145.4}
\]

Use the same dominant three-direction orbit and maximum-degree Mantel partition as
Section 168, AG47, AG58, TL69, and LA90.  Define

\[
 M_0={4s_2^2\over\bar A},
 \qquad
 R_0=\bar A-M_0,
\tag{WO145.5}
\]

and

\[
 d_0={\bar A^2\over4}-s_2^2+{R_0^2\over4}.
\tag{WO145.6}
\]

Each Mantel shore contains dominant-orbit atomic mass at least

\[
\boxed{
 s_0={M_0\over2}-\sqrt{d_0}.
}
\tag{WO145.7}
\]

Trim the three atom populations on each shore until both shore totals are exactly
`s_0`.  Write the resulting masses as

\[
 x_1+x_2+x_3=s_0,
 \qquad
 y_1+y_2+y_3=s_0.
\tag{WO145.8}
\]

Let `M` be the normalized total missing cross-shore pair mass in the trimmed system.
The Mantel partition gives

\[
\boxed{M\le2d_0.}
\tag{WO145.9}
\]

Same-window pairs cannot be strict-rhombus crossings, hence

\[
\boxed{
 p:=x_1y_1+x_2y_2+x_3y_3\le M.
}
\tag{WO145.10}
\]

---

## 2. Majority assignment and the exact weighted-deletion inequality

Majority-assign each label to a shore on which it has weakly larger mass and delete its
minority copy.  If one retained shore were empty, all `s_0` mass on that shore would be
minority mass.  The standard estimate

\[
 \left(\sum_i\min\{x_i,y_i\}\right)^2\le3p
\]

would then give `p>=s_0^2/3`.  The exact certificate verifies
`s_0^2/3>2d_0`, contradicting (WO145.9)--(WO145.10).  Thus both retained shores are
nonempty.

Their label supports are disjoint subsets of three labels.  After relabeling labels and
possibly swapping the shores, `X` contains one center label and `Y` contains one or two
leaf labels.  Thus

\[
 x_1\ge y_1,
 \qquad
 y_2\ge x_2,
 \qquad
 y_3\ge x_3.
\tag{WO145.11}
\]

Set

\[
 u:=x_2+x_3,
 \qquad
 v:=y_1.
\tag{WO145.12}
\]

These are exactly the minority masses deleted from the center shore and leaf shore,
respectively.  Since the shores both have total mass `s_0`,

\[
 x_1=s_0-u,
 \qquad
 y_2+y_3=s_0-v.
\tag{WO145.13}
\]

The majority inequalities imply

\[
 s_0-u=x_1\ge v.
\tag{WO145.14}
\]

Now bound the same-label product from below.  First,

\[
 x_1y_1=(s_0-u)v\ge v^2.
\tag{WO145.15}
\]

Second, because `y_2>=x_2` and `y_3>=x_3`,

\[
 x_2y_2+x_3y_3
 \ge x_2^2+x_3^2
 \ge {u^2\over2}.
\tag{WO145.16}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
 p\ge {u^2\over2}+v^2.
}
\tag{WO145.17}
\]

The weighted deletion appearing in the two-leaf Jensen estimate is

\[
 q:=u+{v\over2}.
\tag{WO145.18}
\]

A direct square completion gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
{9\over4}\left({u^2\over2}+v^2\right)-q^2
&={1\over8}(u-4v)^2\ge0.
\end{aligned}
\tag{WO145.19}
\]

Combining (WO145.17)--(WO145.19),

\[
\boxed{
 q^2\le {9\over4}p,
 \qquad
 q\le {3\over2}\sqrt p.
}
\tag{WO145.20}
\]

The coefficient `9/4` is sharp.  Equality occurs, after scaling, at

\[
 x=y=\left({1\over5},{2\over5},{2\over5}\right),
\]

with the first label assigned to the center shore and the other two to the leaf shore
through tie-breaking.  Thus no improvement of (WO145.20) is available without using
more than the same-label product and the majority signs.

---

## 3. The two leaf pairs consume additional disjoint missing budget

Put

\[
 K:={2\over\sqrt3},
 \qquad
 L:=4+\sqrt2.
\tag{WO145.21}
\]

After majority deletion the center mass is

\[
 a=s_0-u,
\]

and the two leaf masses `b,c` satisfy

\[
 b+c=s_0-v.
\tag{WO145.22}
\]

Every atom population is at most `s_0`, and the exact certificate verifies `s_0<K`.
TL69 therefore applies separately to the two center-to-leaf pairs.  If their normalized
missing masses are `rho_b,rho_c`, then

\[
 \rho_b\ge{(a+b-K)_+^2\over L^2},
 \qquad
 \rho_c\ge{(a+c-K)_+^2\over L^2}.
\tag{WO145.23}
\]

These pair sets are disjoint from one another and from the same-label pair sets counted
by `p`, so

\[
\boxed{M\ge p+\rho_b+\rho_c.}
\tag{WO145.24}
\]

Convexity and Jensen give

\[
\begin{aligned}
\rho_b+\rho_c
&\ge {2\over L^2}
\left(a+{b+c\over2}-K\right)_+^2\\
&={2\over L^2}
\left({3s_0\over2}-K-u-{v\over2}\right)_+^2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{WO145.25}
\]

Define

\[
 B:={3s_0\over2}-K.
\tag{WO145.26}
\]

Using (WO145.20) in (WO145.25), then (WO145.24), yields

\[
\boxed{
 M\ge p+{2\over L^2}
 \left(B-{3\over2}\sqrt p\right)_+^2.
}
\tag{WO145.27}
\]

This is the shared missing-budget inequality.

---

## 4. Exact minimization

Set `z=sqrt(p)>=0`.  On `0<=z<=2B/3`, the right side of (WO145.27) is

\[
 f(z)=z^2+{2\over L^2}\left(B-{3z\over2}\right)^2.
\tag{WO145.28}
\]

The quadratic has its unique minimizer inside this interval and its minimum is

\[
\boxed{
 \min_{z\ge0}f(z)
 ={2B^2\over L^2+9/2}.
}
\tag{WO145.29}
\]

For `z>=2B/3`, the positive-part term vanishes and the same lower bound follows from
`z^2>=4B^2/9`.

Consequently every feasible survivor must satisfy

\[
\boxed{
 2d_0\ge
 {2(3s_0/2-2/\sqrt3)^2
  \over(4+\sqrt2)^2+9/2}.
}
\tag{WO145.30}
\]

---

## 5. Exact contradiction at `eta=0.00145`

The exact interval certificate gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\bar A&=1.906075613727914\ldots,\\
M_0&=1.903176716779643\ldots,\\
R_0&=0.002898896948271\ldots,\\
d_0&=0.001383480095831\ldots,\\
s_0&=0.914393196126001\ldots,\\
B&=0.216889255809750\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{WO145.31}
\]

The shared-payment lower bound is

\[
 {2B^2\over(4+\sqrt2)^2+9/2}
 >0.0027823596626273,
\tag{WO145.32}
\]

while the entire available Mantel missing budget satisfies

\[
 2d_0<0.0027669601916639.
\tag{WO145.33}
\]

The outward-certified gap exceeds

\[
1.5\times10^{-5}.
\tag{WO145.34}
\]

This contradicts (WO145.30), proving (WO145.1).

A high-precision solution of equality in (WO145.30) occurs near

\[
\eta_*=0.00145635040626572\ldots .
\tag{WO145.35}
\]

Thus `0.00145` is close to the ceiling of this weighted-overlap/two-leaf architecture.

---

## 6. Immediate consequences

Every proof-only consequence that depends only on the atomic gap may now use
`g=0.00145`.  In particular,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00145,
}
\tag{WO145.36}
\]

and the rhombic-only transfer gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.0029.
}
\tag{WO145.37}
\]

The correlated stopped-train identity gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q\over D}\ge0.0029.
}
\tag{WO145.38}
\]

The Section-171 and chamber/residual coefficients should be recomputed using
`g=0.00145`.

---

## 7. Scope audit

- The theorem has the same full-core Type-II train-measure scope as audited Section 168,
  TL69, and LA90.
- Same-label products are counted only because same-window trains cannot form actual
  strict-rhombus crossings once the continuity windows are sufficiently small.
- Majority deletion is not free: its weighted cost is bounded directly by the same-label
  missing budget through (WO145.17)--(WO145.20).
- TL69 is applied only to distinct dominant-orbit labels, hence to limiting axes
  separated by exactly `pi/3`.
- Trimming and majority assignment are limiting-mass inequalities, not physical source
  modifications.  Every source point remains in the source.
- No strict-diameter competitor, exact unit triangle, C-II closure, or Level-I closure is
  asserted.

**Disposition:** proof-only WO145 candidate awaiting independent audit.  C-II remains open.
